'use strict';
MANUAL.add('script', [
  {
    name: 'script', kind: 'concept', aliases: ['shebang', '#!', '#!/bin/bash', '#!/usr/bin/env bash', 'shell script', 'script file', 'writing a script', 'executable script', 'chmod +x script', 'running a script', './script.sh', 'bash script.sh', 'sh script.sh', 'script structure', 'comments', '#', 'scripts', 'guion', 'script.sh', 'script template', 'bash vs sh', 'strict mode', 'set -euo pipefail'],
    summary: 'How a shell script is written, made executable and run: shebang, comments, arguments, exit status and the usual structure.',
    synopsis: ['#!/bin/bash                 # first line: the interpreter', 'chmod +x script.sh && ./script.sh ARGS', 'bash script.sh ARGS        # no execute permission needed', 'source script.sh            # run in the current shell'],
    desc: [
      'A shell **script** is a text file with commands, run by an interpreter. The **first line**, the **shebang** `#!/bin/bash` (or the portable `#!/usr/bin/env bash`), tells the kernel which program must read the rest when the file is executed directly. The shebang must be the very first bytes of the file, with no space before `#`. A `#` anywhere else starts a **comment** until the end of the line.',
      '**Three ways to run it**: (1) `./script.sh` — needs the **execute** permission (`chmod +x`) and a path (a name without `/` is searched in `$PATH`, which normally does not contain the current directory); it runs in a **new child process**. (2) `bash script.sh` — only needs **read** permission and ignores the shebang. (3) `source script.sh` or `. script.sh` — runs the commands in the **current shell**, so variables and `cd` remain afterwards.',
      'A script receives its **arguments** in `$1`, `$2`... (`$#` how many, `"$@"` all of them, `$0` its own name) and ends with an **exit status**: that of the last command, or the number given to `exit N`. `0` means success. The caller reads it in `$?`. Well-behaved scripts check their arguments, print usage and errors to **stderr** (`>&2`), quote their variables and return a meaningful status.',
      '`set -e` (exit on the first failing command), `set -u` (error on unset variables) and `set -o pipefail` (a pipeline fails if any part fails) make a script safer ("strict mode": `set -euo pipefail`); but they have subtleties, so use them knowing what they do (see `set`).',
    ],
    sections: [{
      title: 'A typical script skeleton', code: '#!/bin/bash\n# summary: what the script does\n# usage:   script.sh [-v] FILE...\n\nset -u                                   # unset variables are errors\n\nusage() { echo "usage: $0 [-v] FILE..." >&2; exit 2; }\n\n[ $# -ge 1 ] || usage                    # check the arguments\n\nfor f in "$@"; do                        # quote everything\n    if [ ! -r "$f" ]; then\n        echo "$0: cannot read $f" >&2    # errors on stderr\n        status=1\n        continue\n    fi\n    echo "$f: $(wc -l < "$f") lines"\ndone\n\nexit "${status:-0}"                      # 0 unless something failed',
    }],
    examples: [
      { title: 'The smallest script', cmd: 'cat > hello.sh << \'EOF\'\n#!/bin/bash\necho "Hello from a script"\nEOF\nchmod +x hello.sh\n./hello.sh' },
      { title: 'Without execute permission', cmd: 'printf \'#!/bin/bash\\necho hi\\n\' > s.sh\n./s.sh; echo "status: $?"\nbash s.sh; echo "status: $?"', fails: true, note: '`Permission denied` (126) for `./s.sh`; `bash s.sh` works.' },
      { title: 'The current directory is not in PATH', cmd: 'printf \'#!/bin/bash\\necho hi\\n\' > s.sh; chmod +x s.sh\ns.sh; echo "status: $?"\n./s.sh', fails: true, note: 'Without the `./` the shell only searches `$PATH`.' },
      { title: 'A script runs in a child process', cmd: 'printf \'cd /tmp\\nx=changed\\n\' > s.sh\nx=original\nbash s.sh\necho "x=$x, still in $(basename "$PWD")"\nsource s.sh\necho "x=$x, now in $PWD"', note: 'The child cannot change your directory or variables; `source` runs it in your shell.' },
      { title: 'Arguments, name and status', cmd: 'cat > args.sh << \'EOF\'\n#!/bin/bash\necho "I am $0 and I got $# argument(s): $*"\n[ $# -ge 2 ] && exit 0\nexit 3\nEOF\nbash args.sh a b; echo "status: $?"\nbash args.sh a; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'The shebang decides the interpreter', cmd: 'printf \'#!/usr/bin/env python3\\nprint("hello from python")\\n\' > p.py\nchmod +x p.py\n./p.py 2>&1 | head -n 1', note: 'Without python installed you would see "env: ‘python3’: No such file or directory". Any program that reads a file can be an interpreter (`awk`, `perl`...).' },
      { title: 'Comments', cmd: '# a whole-line comment\necho "visible" # a trailing comment\necho "a # inside quotes is not a comment"' },
      { title: 'Windows line endings break the shebang', cmd: 'printf \'#!/bin/bash\\r\\necho hi\\r\\n\' > win.sh\nchmod +x win.sh\n./win.sh 2>&1 | sed "s/\\r//"; echo "status: ${PIPESTATUS[0]}"\ntr -d "\\r" < win.sh > ok.sh; chmod +x ok.sh; ./ok.sh', fails: true, note: 'The `\\r` becomes part of the interpreter name (`/bin/bash^M`): "required file not found" (older systems say "bad interpreter"). Fix with `tr -d \'\\r\'` or `dos2unix`.' },
      { title: 'Safe exit-status handling', cmd: 'cat > check.sh << \'EOF\'\n#!/bin/bash\nif ! grep -q "^root:" /etc/passwd; then\n    echo "no root?" >&2\n    exit 1\nfi\necho "root exists"\nEOF\nbash check.sh; echo "status: $?"' },
    ],
    see: ['exit-status', 'test', 'functions', 'special-parameters', 'set', 'source', 'bash', 'patterns-usage'],
  },
  {
    name: 'exit-status', kind: 'concept', aliases: ['exit codes', 'exit code', 'exit status', 'return code', 'return status', 'status', '$? exit', 'success and failure', '0 = success', 'códigos de salida', 'exit codes: 0 = success', '126', '127', '128+n', 'command not found 127', 'exit 1', 'exit 0', 'convention exit codes', 'exit statuses'],
    summary: 'Every command ends with a number from 0 to 255: 0 means success, anything else a kind of failure. `$?` holds the last one.',
    synopsis: ['COMMAND; echo $?', 'if COMMAND; then ...; fi', 'COMMAND1 && COMMAND2 || COMMAND3', 'exit N    return N'],
    desc: [
      'When a command finishes it returns an **exit status** (also *exit code* or *return code*) to its parent, an integer 0–255. By convention **0 = success** and **non-zero = failure** (the number says what kind: 1 generic, 2 misuse). The shell stores the status of the last command in **`$?`**. This is the **opposite** of C, where 0 is false: in the shell **0 is "true"**.',
      'The shell *uses* statuses as conditions: `if`, `while`, `until`, `&&`, `||` and `!` all test a **command** and run depending on whether it succeeded: `if grep -q x file; then ...`. `test` / `[ ]` / `[[ ]]` / `(( ))` are commands whose only job is to return a status. The status of a **pipeline** is that of its last command (unless `pipefail`); of a **function** that of its last command or `return N`; of a **script** that of its last command or `exit N`.',
      'Common values: **0** success; **1** general error (also `grep`: no match; `diff`: differ; `test`: false); **2** misuse of a command (bad option or arguments); **126** found but not executable (permission denied); **127** command not found; **128+N** terminated by signal N (130 = Ctrl-C, 137 = `kill -9`, 143 = `kill`/SIGTERM); **255** out of range / `exit -1`.',
    ],
    sections: [{
      title: 'Where each value comes from', table: [
        ['Status', 'Meaning', 'Example'],
        ['`0`', 'success', '`true`, `ls /`, `grep` found a line'],
        ['`1`', 'failure / false', '`false`, `grep` found nothing, `[ 1 -eq 2 ]`'],
        ['`2`', 'wrong usage', '`ls --nooption`, `grep` error (missing file)'],
        ['`126`', 'cannot execute', '`./file` without the x permission'],
        ['`127`', 'command not found', '`nosuchcommand`'],
        ['`128+N`', 'killed by signal N', '130 Ctrl-C (2), 137 SIGKILL (9), 143 SIGTERM (15)'],
      ],
    }],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Read $? right after the command', cmd: 'true; echo "true: $?"\nfalse; echo "false: $?"\nls /nonexistent 2>/dev/null; echo "ls: $?"' },
      { title: '$? changes with every command', cmd: 'false\necho "first: $?"\necho "second: $?"', note: 'The first `echo` succeeded, so `$?` is 0 for the second. Save it if you need it: `status=$?`.' },
      { title: 'Command not found and not executable', cmd: 'nosuchcommand 2>/dev/null; echo "not found: $?"\ntouch s.sh\n./s.sh 2>/dev/null; echo "not executable: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'Killed by signals', cmd: 'sleep 30 &\nkill $!\nwait $! 2>/dev/null; echo "SIGTERM: $?"\nsleep 30 &\nkill -9 $!\nwait $! 2>/dev/null; echo "SIGKILL: $?"' },
      { title: 'A command is a condition', cmd: 'if grep -q root /etc/passwd; then echo "found"; else echo "not found"; fi\nif ! ls /nonexistent > /dev/null 2>&1; then echo "does not exist"; fi' },
      { title: 'Several outcomes with case', cmd: 'grep -q root /nonexistent 2>/dev/null\ncase $? in\n  0) echo "match" ;;\n  1) echo "no match" ;;\n  *) echo "error" ;;\nesac' },
      { title: 'Chain: && and ||', cmd: 'mkdir d && cd d && echo "in $(basename "$PWD")"\nfalse || echo "after a failure"\ntrue && echo "after a success"' },
      { title: 'A script returns its own status', cmd: 'printf \'echo working\\nexit 42\\n\' > s.sh\nbash s.sh\necho "script returned $?"' },
      { title: 'A function returns a status', cmd: 'is_even() { [ $(($1 % 2)) -eq 0 ]; }\nis_even 4 && echo "4 is even"\nis_even 7 || echo "7 is odd"' },
      { title: 'Status of a pipeline', cmd: 'false | true; echo "last wins: $?"\nset -o pipefail\nfalse | true; echo "pipefail: $?"' },
      { title: 'The status of an assignment is that of the substitution', cmd: 'x=$(false); echo "$?"\nx=$(true); echo "$?"\nlocal_y=$(exit 5); echo "$?"' },
      { title: 'Use a meaningful exit code', cmd: 'cat > c.sh << \'EOF\'\n#!/bin/bash\n[ $# -eq 1 ] || { echo "usage: $0 FILE" >&2; exit 2; }\n[ -e "$1" ] || { echo "$1: not found" >&2; exit 1; }\necho "$1 exists"\nEOF\nbash c.sh; echo "status: $?"\nbash c.sh nofile; echo "status: $?"\nbash c.sh /etc/passwd; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
    ],
    notes: ['`exit` with a number above 255 wraps around (`exit 256` is 0).', 'Do not write `if [ $? -eq 0 ]` after a command: test the command directly (`if cmd; then`).', 'The status of `[ ... ]` is 0 when the condition is **true**.'],
    see: ['test', 'if', 'exit', 'special-parameters', 'trap', 'kill'],
  },
  {
    name: 'exit', kind: 'builtin', aliases: ['exit N', 'return', 'return N', 'exit in function', 'exit vs return'],
    summary: 'End the shell or script with a status (`exit`); end a function with a status (`return`).',
    synopsis: ['exit [N]', 'return [N]'],
    desc: ['`exit N` ends the current shell or script and gives **N** (0–255) as its exit status; without N it uses the status of the last command. In an interactive shell it closes the terminal. Inside a **subshell** `( ... )` or `$( ... )` it only ends that subshell. `exit` also runs the `EXIT` trap. **`return N`** is for **functions** (and sourced scripts): it leaves the function with the status N without ending the script; outside of a function it is an error. Inside a function, `exit` ends the **whole script**.'],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Exit with a status', cmd: 'bash -c \'echo before; exit 3; echo never\'; echo "status: $?"' },
      { title: 'No number: the last status', cmd: 'bash -c \'false; exit\'; echo "status: $?"\nbash -c \'true; exit\'; echo "status: $?"' },
      { title: 'return leaves only the function', cmd: 'f() { echo "in f"; return 7; echo "never"; }\nf; echo "f returned $?"\necho "script goes on"' },
      { title: 'exit inside a function ends the script', cmd: 'bash -c \'g() { echo "in g"; exit 9; }; g; echo "never printed"\'; echo "status: $?"' },
      { title: 'exit inside a subshell only ends it', cmd: '( exit 5 ); echo "subshell returned $?"\necho "still running"' },
      { title: 'Stop on a bad argument', cmd: 'cat > s.sh << \'EOF\'\n[ -n "$1" ] || { echo "missing argument" >&2; exit 1; }\necho "ok: $1"\nEOF\nbash s.sh; echo "status: $?"\nbash s.sh hi; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'Values wrap around', cmd: 'bash -c "exit 256"; echo "$?"\nbash -c "exit 257"; echo "$?"\nbash -c "exit -1"; echo "$?"' },
      { title: 'The EXIT trap runs on exit', cmd: 'bash -c \'trap "echo cleanup" EXIT; echo working; exit 4\'; echo "status: $?"' },
    ],
    exit: ['N (modulo 256).'],
    see: ['exit-status', 'functions', 'trap', 'script'],
  },
  {
    name: 'test', kind: 'builtin', aliases: ['[', '[ ]', '[[', '[[ ]]', 'test -e', 'test -f', 'test -d', 'test -r', 'test -w', 'test -x', 'test -s', 'test -z', 'test -n', 'test -eq', 'test -ne', 'test -lt', 'test -le', 'test -gt', 'test -ge', 'test -L', 'test -h', 'test -nt', 'test -ot', 'test -ef', 'test -e -d', 'test -r -w -x', 'test -lt -gt', 'test -b', 'test -c', 'test -p', 'test -S', 'test -O', 'test -G', 'test -u', 'test -g', 'test -k', 'test -t', 'test -v', 'test -a', 'test -o', '! test', '==', '!=', '=', '[[ -f ]]', '[[ == ]]', '[ -f ]', 'conditions', 'condition', 'conditional expressions', 'file tests', 'string tests', 'numeric tests', 'comparison', 'string comparison', 'numeric comparison', '-eq -ne -lt -le -gt -ge', 'file test operators', 'test operators', 'test -f -d', '[ -z ]', '[ -n ]', 'integer comparison', 'condiciones'],
    summary: 'Evaluate a condition (file, string or number tests) and return success or failure: `test`, `[ ]` and `[[ ]]`.',
    synopsis: ['test EXPRESSION', '[ EXPRESSION ]          # same, POSIX; spaces are mandatory', '[[ EXPRESSION ]]        # bash: safer, with patterns and =~'],
    desc: [
      '`test EXPRESSION` and `[ EXPRESSION ]` (same program; the closing `]` is just a required last argument) evaluate EXPRESSION and **exit with 0 if it is true, 1 if false** (2 on a syntax error). They print nothing: you use them as the condition of `if`, `while`, `&&`, `||`. The **spaces** around `[`, `]` and every operator are mandatory (`[ $a = $b ]`, never `[$a=$b]`). **`[[ ... ]]`** is the bash keyword that is safer and richer (no word splitting or globbing of the variables inside, pattern matching with `==`, regex with `=~`, `&&` and `||` inside, `<`/`>` string ordering) but is **not POSIX**.',
      'In **`[ ]`** quote every variable (`[ "$x" = "yes" ]`): an empty or space-containing variable would otherwise disappear or split and give "too many arguments" / "unary operator expected". Inside `[ ]` use `-a` / `-o` for and/or (or better, two tests joined with `&&` / `||`); `<` and `>` must be escaped (`\\<`); in **`[[ ]]`** quoting is not required for variables (but is on the right of `==` if you want a literal, not a pattern).',
      'For **arithmetic** comparisons use `-eq -ne -lt -le -gt -ge` in `[ ]`/`[[ ]]`, or the arithmetic command `(( a < b ))`. The operators `=`, `==`, `!=`, `<`, `>` compare **strings** (`[ 10 \\> 9 ]` is false!).',
    ],
    sections: [
      { title: 'File tests', table: [
        ['Test', 'True if FILE...'],
        ['`-e FILE`', 'exists (any type)'],
        ['`-f FILE`', 'exists and is a **regular file**'],
        ['`-d FILE`', 'exists and is a **directory**'],
        ['`-L FILE` / `-h FILE`', 'is a symbolic link'],
        ['`-r` / `-w` / `-x FILE`', 'exists and is readable / writable / executable by **you**'],
        ['`-s FILE`', 'exists and has a **size greater than zero** (not empty)'],
        ['`-b` / `-c` / `-p` / `-S FILE`', 'is a block device / character device / named pipe / socket'],
        ['`-O` / `-G FILE`', 'is owned by your user / your group'],
        ['`-u` / `-g` / `-k FILE`', 'has the setuid / setgid / sticky bit'],
        ['`F1 -nt F2` / `F1 -ot F2`', 'F1 is **newer** / **older** than F2 (modification time)'],
        ['`F1 -ef F2`', 'F1 and F2 are the **same file** (same device and inode: hard links)'],
        ['`-t FD`', 'descriptor FD is open on a terminal (`[ -t 1 ]`)'],
      ] },
      { title: 'String tests', table: [
        ['Test', 'True if...'],
        ['`-z STR`', 'STR is **empty** (length zero)'],
        ['`-n STR`', 'STR is **not empty** (or just `[ "$s" ]`)'],
        ['`S1 = S2` / `S1 == S2`', 'the strings are **equal** (in `[[ ]]` the right side is a **pattern** unless quoted)'],
        ['`S1 != S2`', 'the strings are different'],
        ['`S1 < S2` / `S1 > S2`', 'S1 sorts before / after S2 (in `[ ]` write `\\<` `\\>`)'],
        ['`[[ S =~ REGEX ]]`', 'S matches the extended regex (bash only; groups in `BASH_REMATCH`)'],
        ['`-v VAR`', 'the variable VAR is set (bash)'],
      ] },
      { title: 'Numeric tests', table: [
        ['Test', 'True if...'],
        ['`N1 -eq N2`', 'equal'],
        ['`N1 -ne N2`', 'not equal'],
        ['`N1 -lt N2` / `-le`', 'less than / less or equal'],
        ['`N1 -gt N2` / `-ge`', 'greater than / greater or equal'],
      ] },
      { title: 'Combining', table: [
        ['Form', 'Meaning'],
        ['`! EXPR`', 'negation'],
        ['`[ A ] && [ B ]`', 'and (recommended)'],
        ['`[ A ] || [ B ]`', 'or (recommended)'],
        ['`[ A -a B ]` / `[ A -o B ]`', 'and / or inside one test (obsolete, ambiguous)'],
        ['`[[ A && B ]]` / `[[ A || B ]]`', 'and / or inside `[[ ]]`'],
        ['`( EXPR )`', 'grouping (escape in `[ ]`: `\\( \\)`; plain in `[[ ]]`)'],
      ] },
    ],
    examples: [
      { title: 'The exit status is the result', cmd: 'test 5 -gt 3; echo "5 > 3: $?"\n[ 2 -gt 3 ]; echo "2 > 3: $?"' },
      { title: 'File tests', cmd: 'touch file; mkdir dir; ln -s file link; : > empty; echo data > full\n[ -e file ] && echo "file exists"\n[ -f dir ] || echo "dir is not a regular file"\n[ -d dir ] && echo "dir is a directory"\n[ -L link ] && echo "link is a symlink"\n[ -s full ] && echo "full is not empty"\n[ -s empty ] || echo "empty is empty"' },
      { title: 'Permissions tests', cmd: 'touch f; chmod 640 f\n[ -r f ] && echo readable\n[ -w f ] && echo writable\n[ -x f ] || echo "not executable"\nchmod u+x f; [ -x f ] && echo "now executable"' },
      { title: 'Compare dates and identity', cmd: 'touch -d "2024-01-01" old; touch -d "2024-06-01" new; ln new alias\n[ new -nt old ] && echo "new is newer"\n[ old -ot new ] && echo "old is older"\n[ new -ef alias ] && echo "same file"' },
      { title: 'Strings', cmd: 'a=hello; b=hello; c=world\n[ "$a" = "$b" ] && echo "a equals b"\n[ "$a" != "$c" ] && echo "a differs from c"\n[ -z "" ] && echo "empty string"\n[ -n "$a" ] && echo "a is not empty"' },
      { title: 'Numbers', cmd: 'n=15\n[ "$n" -gt 10 ] && echo "greater than 10"\n[ "$n" -le 15 ] && echo "at most 15"\n[ "$n" -eq 15 ] && echo "exactly 15"\n[ "$n" -ne 3 ] && echo "not 3"' },
      { title: 'String comparison is not numeric', cmd: '[ "10" \\> "9" ] && echo "string: 10 > 9" || echo "string: 10 < 9"\n[ 10 -gt 9 ] && echo "numeric: 10 > 9"' },
      { title: 'Quote your variables in [ ]', cmd: 'v=""\n[ $v = x ]; echo "unquoted empty: $?"\n[ "$v" = x ]; echo "quoted empty: $?"\nw="two words"\n[ $w = x ]; echo "unquoted spaces: $?"', fails: true, note: 'Unquoted, the empty variable vanishes (`[ = x ]` is a syntax error) and the spaces split it (`too many arguments`).' },
      { title: 'Combine conditions', cmd: 'n=7\n[ "$n" -ge 1 ] && [ "$n" -le 10 ] && echo "between 1 and 10"\n[ "$n" -lt 0 ] || [ "$n" -gt 5 ] && echo "negative or above 5"\n[ ! -e /nonexistent ] && echo "does not exist"\n[ "$n" -gt 5 -a "$n" -lt 9 ] && echo "old-style and"' },
      { title: '[[ ]] is safer: no quoting needed', cmd: 'v=""\n[[ $v = x ]]; echo "status: $?"\nw="two words"\n[[ $w == "two words" ]] && echo "equal"', fails: true },
      { title: '[[ ]] patterns and regex', cmd: 'f=report.txt\n[[ $f == *.txt ]] && echo "a .txt file"\n[[ $f == "*.txt" ]] || echo "quoted: literal comparison"\n[[ $f =~ ^[a-z]+\\.txt$ ]] && echo "regex match"\n[[ 2024-05-17 =~ ^([0-9]{4})-([0-9]{2}) ]] && echo "year ${BASH_REMATCH[1]} month ${BASH_REMATCH[2]}"' },
      { title: '[[ ]] with && and || inside', cmd: 'a=5; b=hello\n[[ $a -gt 3 && $b == h* ]] && echo "both true"\n[[ $a -gt 9 || $b == h* ]] && echo "one is true"' },
      { title: 'String ordering', cmd: '[[ apple < banana ]] && echo "apple sorts before banana"\n[ "b" \\> "a" ] && echo "b after a"' },
      { title: 'Is the variable set?', cmd: 'unset v\n[[ -v v ]] || echo "v is unset"\nv=""\n[[ -v v ]] && echo "v is set (empty)"\n[ -z "$v" ] && echo "but its value is empty"' },
      { title: 'Validate an integer', cmd: 'for v in 42 -7 4x ""; do [[ $v =~ ^-?[0-9]+$ ]] && echo "[$v] integer" || echo "[$v] not an integer"; done' },
      { title: 'Test and act in one line', cmd: '[ -d /tmp ] && echo "/tmp exists" || echo "no /tmp"\n[ -f /nonexistent ] || echo "missing"', note: '`A && B || C` is "if A then B else C" — except that C also runs if B fails.' },
      { title: 'Syntax errors give status 2', cmd: '[ 1 -eq ]; echo "status: $?"\n[ a b ] 2>/dev/null; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'Is the output a terminal?', cmd: '[ -t 1 ] && echo "terminal" || echo "not a terminal (a pipe or file)"' },
      { title: 'The classic error: missing spaces', cmd: '[1 -eq 1]; echo "status: $?"\n[ 1 -eq 1 ]; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'test and [ are the same', cmd: 'test -d /tmp && echo yes\n[ -d /tmp ] && echo yes\nls -l /usr/bin/[ /usr/bin/test | wc -l' },
    ],
    exit: ['0 expression true, 1 false, 2 invalid expression.'],
    notes: [
      '`=` for strings, `-eq` for numbers: `[ 01 = 1 ]` is false, `[ 01 -eq 1 ]` is true.',
      'A missing closing `]` or a missing space gives `[: missing ]` / `command not found`.',
      '`[ -f $file ]` fails for names with spaces if unquoted: always `"$file"`.',
      '`-a` and `-o` inside `[ ]` can be misparsed when an operand is `!` or `(`: use `&&` / `||` between separate tests.',
      'To test a command\'s result do not use `[ ]`: `if grep -q x f; then`.',
    ],
    see: ['if', 'exit-status', 'regex', 'arithmetic', 'case', 'while'],
  },
  {
    name: 'if', kind: 'syntax', aliases: ['if then else', 'if/elif/else', 'elif', 'else', 'fi', 'then', 'if [ ]', 'if ! cmd', 'conditional', 'conditionals', 'if statement', 'if cmd; then', 'if elif else fi', 'if -f', 'if test', 'if [[ ]]', 'if (( ))', 'condicional', '&& ||', '&&', '||', 'if a; then b; fi', 'and or lists', 'and-or', 'cmd1 && cmd2', 'cmd1 || cmd2', 'a && b || c', '! (negation)', '!'],
    summary: 'Conditional execution: `if COMMAND; then ...; elif ...; else ...; fi`, and the short forms `&&` / `||`.',
    synopsis: ['if COMMANDS; then COMMANDS; [elif COMMANDS; then COMMANDS;]... [else COMMANDS;] fi', 'COMMAND1 && COMMAND2        COMMAND1 || COMMAND2', '! COMMAND'],
    desc: [
      '`if` runs the **command list** after `if` and looks at its **exit status**: if it is **0** (success) the `then` branch runs; otherwise each `elif` is tried in turn, and finally the `else` branch (if present). The condition is *any command*, not only `[ ]`: `if grep -q x f`, `if mkdir d`, `if ! cmd`, `if [[ ... ]]`, `if (( ... ))`. `fi` closes the block. Newlines can replace the `;` before `then`.',
      'The **short forms**: `A && B` runs B only if A **succeeded** (status 0); `A || B` runs B only if A **failed**. They are *and-or lists* evaluated left to right and are perfect for guards: `[ -f f ] || exit 1`, `cd dir || exit`, `mkdir -p d && cd d`. `A && B || C` looks like if-then-else but **C also runs when B fails**; use a real `if` when that matters. `! cmd` negates the status. `;` simply separates commands (the next runs regardless).',
    ],
    examples: [
      { title: 'if / else', cmd: 'n=7\nif [ "$n" -gt 5 ]; then\n  echo "big"\nelse\n  echo "small"\nfi' },
      { title: 'if / elif / else', cmd: 'score=72\nif [ "$score" -ge 90 ]; then echo A\nelif [ "$score" -ge 70 ]; then echo B\nelif [ "$score" -ge 50 ]; then echo C\nelse echo F\nfi' },
      { title: 'The condition is a command', cmd: 'if grep -q "^root:" /etc/passwd; then echo "root is defined"; fi\nif mkdir newdir 2>/dev/null; then echo "created"; else echo "already existed"; fi\nif mkdir newdir 2>/dev/null; then echo "created"; else echo "already existed"; fi' },
      { title: 'Negation', cmd: 'if ! [ -e /nonexistent ]; then echo "missing"; fi\nif ! grep -q zzz /etc/passwd; then echo "no zzz user"; fi' },
      { title: 'Several conditions', cmd: 'age=25; member=yes\nif [ "$age" -ge 18 ] && [ "$member" = yes ]; then echo "welcome"; fi\nif [[ $age -lt 12 || $age -gt 65 ]]; then echo "discount"; else echo "full price"; fi' },
      { title: 'Arithmetic condition', cmd: 'n=8\nif (( n % 2 == 0 )); then echo "$n is even"; fi\nif (( n > 5 && n < 10 )); then echo "between 5 and 10"; fi' },
      { title: 'Check arguments', cmd: 'cat > s.sh << \'EOF\'\nif [ $# -eq 0 ]; then\n  echo "usage: $0 NAME" >&2\n  exit 1\nfi\necho "Hello, $1"\nEOF\nbash s.sh; echo "status: $?"\nbash s.sh Ana', fails: true },
      { title: 'Guard with &&', cmd: 'mkdir work && cd work && echo "now in $(basename "$PWD")"\nmkdir work 2>/dev/null && echo "not shown"; echo "continues"' },
      { title: 'Default with ||', cmd: 'cd /nonexistent 2>/dev/null || echo "could not cd"\ngrep -q zzz /etc/passwd || echo "zzz not found"' },
      { title: 'A && B || C is not if-then-else', cmd: 'true && false || echo "C ran although the condition was true"', note: 'B failed, so C ran. Use a real `if` when B can fail.' },
      { title: 'Exit if something is wrong', cmd: 'cat > s.sh << \'EOF\'\n[ -r "$1" ] || { echo "cannot read $1" >&2; exit 1; }\necho "reading $1"\nEOF\nbash s.sh /nonexistent; echo "status: $?"\nbash s.sh /etc/passwd; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'Test a string against a pattern', cmd: 'file=photo.jpg\nif [[ $file == *.jpg || $file == *.png ]]; then echo "image"; fi' },
      { title: 'Nested ifs', cmd: 'f=/etc/passwd\nif [ -e "$f" ]; then\n  if [ -r "$f" ]; then echo "readable"; else echo "not readable"; fi\nelse\n  echo "missing"\nfi' },
      { title: 'One-liner syntax', cmd: 'if true; then echo yes; fi; if false; then echo yes; else echo no; fi' },
      { title: 'An empty then is an error', cmd: 'if true; then\nfi', fails: true, note: 'A branch needs at least one command: use `:` (do nothing).' },
    ],
    see: ['test', 'case', 'exit-status', 'while', 'arithmetic'],
  },
  {
    name: 'case', kind: 'syntax', aliases: ['esac', 'case ... esac', 'case statement', 'case patterns', ';;', 'case in', ';&', 'case $1 in', 'case with patterns', 'switch'],
    summary: 'Multi-way branch on a value, matching glob patterns: `case WORD in PATTERN) ...;; esac`.',
    synopsis: ['case WORD in\\n  PATTERN1|PATTERN2) COMMANDS ;;\\n  PATTERN3) COMMANDS ;;\\n  *) DEFAULT ;;\\nesac'],
    desc: ['`case` compares WORD with each PATTERN in order and runs the commands of the **first** pattern that matches, up to `;;`. Patterns are **shell globs** (not regular expressions): `*` any string, `?` one character, `[abc]` a set, and `|` separates alternatives (`yes|y|Y)`). `*)` as the last pattern is the **default**. Quote the word if it can contain spaces; patterns are not quoted unless you want a literal match. The status is that of the last command executed (0 if no pattern matched). It is the right tool when you test one variable against many values (menu options, options of a script, file types by extension). `;&` falls through to the next branch and `;;&` keeps testing further patterns (bash 4).'],
    examples: [
      { title: 'A simple menu', cmd: 'answer=yes\ncase "$answer" in\n  yes) echo "agreed" ;;\n  no)  echo "refused" ;;\n  *)   echo "unknown answer" ;;\nesac' },
      { title: 'Alternatives with |', cmd: 'for a in y Y yes YES maybe; do\n  case $a in\n    y|Y|yes|YES) echo "$a: affirmative" ;;\n    *)           echo "$a: not clear" ;;\n  esac\ndone' },
      { title: 'Patterns: extensions', cmd: 'for f in a.txt b.jpg c.tar.gz d; do\n  case $f in\n    *.txt)        echo "$f: text" ;;\n    *.jpg|*.png)  echo "$f: image" ;;\n    *.tar.gz|*.tgz) echo "$f: archive" ;;\n    *)            echo "$f: other" ;;\n  esac\ndone' },
      { title: 'Character classes and lengths', cmd: 'for c in a Z 5 "?" ab; do\n  case $c in\n    [a-z]) echo "$c: lower case letter" ;;\n    [A-Z]) echo "$c: upper case letter" ;;\n    [0-9]) echo "$c: digit" ;;\n    ?)     echo "$c: another single character" ;;\n    *)     echo "$c: longer than one character" ;;\n  esac\ndone' },
      { title: 'Options of a script', cmd: 'cat > s.sh << \'EOF\'\ncase "$1" in\n  start)   echo "starting" ;;\n  stop)    echo "stopping" ;;\n  restart) echo "restarting" ;;\n  *)       echo "usage: $0 {start|stop|restart}" >&2; exit 1 ;;\nesac\nEOF\nbash s.sh start\nbash s.sh bogus; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'Check the status of a command', cmd: 'grep -q root /etc/passwd\ncase $? in\n  0) echo "found" ;;\n  1) echo "not found" ;;\n  *) echo "error" ;;\nesac' },
      { title: 'Case-insensitive match', cmd: 'shopt -s nocasematch\ncase "Hello" in\n  hello) echo "matches ignoring case" ;;\nesac' },
      { title: 'Patterns are globs, not regexes', cmd: 'case "abc123" in\n  [a-z]*[0-9]) echo "starts with a letter, ends with a digit" ;;\nesac\ncase "a.b" in\n  a.b) echo "literal dot" ;;\nesac\ncase "aXb" in\n  a.b) echo "regex-like" ;;\n  *) echo "a dot in a glob is a literal dot" ;;\nesac' },
      { title: 'Quote a pattern to match literally', cmd: 'case "*" in\n  "*") echo "the star character" ;;\nesac\ncase "anything" in\n  *) echo "any string" ;;\nesac' },
      { title: 'Fall-through (;&)', cmd: 'case b in\n  a) echo "a" ;;\n  b) echo "b" ;&\n  c) echo "c (falls through)" ;;\nesac' },
      { title: 'Empty value and no match', cmd: 'v=""\ncase "$v" in\n  "") echo "empty" ;;\n  *) echo "not empty" ;;\nesac\ncase x in y) echo no ;; esac; echo "status without a match: $?"' },
    ],
    see: ['if', 'test', 'glob', 'getopts', 'while'],
  },
  {
    name: 'for', kind: 'syntax', aliases: ['for in', 'for ... in', 'for loop', 'for (( ))', 'for i in', 'for f in *', 'for "$@"', 'for in "$@"', 'for arg', 'for loops', 'do done', 'done', 'do', 'for i in $(seq)', 'for i in {1..5}', 'iteration', 'bucle for', 'for var in list', 'loop over files', 'loops', 'loop'],
    summary: 'Loop over a list of words (`for x in LIST`) or with a C-style counter (`for ((i=0; i<n; i++))`).',
    synopsis: ['for NAME in WORD...; do COMMANDS; done', 'for NAME; do COMMANDS; done          # over "$@"', 'for (( INIT; CONDITION; STEP )); do COMMANDS; done'],
    desc: ['`for NAME in LIST` runs the body once for each word of LIST, setting NAME to that word. The list is **expanded first** — so it can be literal words (`a b c`), a **glob** (`*.txt`), a **brace sequence** (`{1..5}`), a command substitution (`$(seq 1 5)`), an array (`"${arr[@]}"`) or the arguments (`"$@"`; with `for NAME` and no `in` this is the default). **Quote** the variable inside the body (`"$f"`), and do not loop over `$(ls)` (it splits names with spaces): use a glob. If a glob matches nothing, the loop runs once with the literal pattern: check `[ -e "$f" ] || continue`.', 'The **C-style** loop `for ((i=0; i<10; i++))` uses an arithmetic counter and, unlike braces, accepts variables as limits. `break` leaves the loop and `continue` jumps to the next iteration (`break N` leaves N levels). The loop variable keeps its last value after the loop. The status of the loop is that of the last command executed in the body.'],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Over words', cmd: 'for fruit in apple pear fig; do\n  echo "I like $fruit"\ndone' },
      { title: 'Over files (a glob)', cmd: 'touch a.txt b.txt "c d.txt"\nfor f in *.txt; do\n  echo "file: [$f] has $(wc -c < "$f") bytes"\ndone', note: 'The name with a space stays whole because `*.txt` is a glob and `$f` is quoted.' },
      { title: 'Over a sequence', cmd: 'for i in {1..5}; do echo -n "$i "; done; echo\nfor i in $(seq 10 -3 1); do echo -n "$i "; done; echo\nfor c in {a..e}; do echo -n "$c"; done; echo' },
      { title: 'C-style', cmd: 'n=5\nfor (( i=1; i<=n; i++ )); do echo -n "$((i*i)) "; done; echo\nfor (( i=0, j=10; i<j; i+=3, j-=3 )); do echo "i=$i j=$j"; done' },
      { title: 'Over the arguments', cmd: 'cat > s.sh << \'EOF\'\nfor arg in "$@"; do echo "arg: [$arg]"; done\nEOF\nbash s.sh one "two words" three' },
      { title: 'Over an array', cmd: 'files=("my file" other)\nfor f in "${files[@]}"; do echo "[$f]"; done' },
      { title: 'Over lines of a command', cmd: 'printf "ana 30\\nluis 25\\n" > people\nfor name in $(cut -d" " -f1 people); do echo "hello $name"; done', note: 'Fine for words without spaces; for lines use `while read`.' },
      { title: 'break and continue', cmd: 'for i in 1 2 3 4 5 6; do\n  [ $i -eq 2 ] && continue\n  [ $i -eq 5 ] && break\n  echo "i=$i"\ndone' },
      { title: 'A glob without matches', cmd: 'for f in *.nosuch; do echo "[$f]"; done\nfor f in *.nosuch; do [ -e "$f" ] || continue; echo "never"; done; echo "skipped"', note: 'The pattern stays literal when nothing matches: guard with `[ -e "$f" ]` or `shopt -s nullglob`.' },
      { title: 'Accumulate a result', cmd: 'total=0\nfor n in 4 8 15 16 23 42; do total=$((total + n)); done\necho "total: $total"' },
      { title: 'Nested loops and break 2', cmd: 'for i in 1 2 3; do\n  for j in a b c; do\n    [ "$i$j" = 2b ] && break 2\n    echo -n "$i$j "\n  done\ndone; echo' },
      { title: 'Rename files in a loop', cmd: 'touch a.txt b.txt\nfor f in *.txt; do mv "$f" "${f%.txt}.bak"; done\nls' },
      { title: 'Loop over directories only', cmd: 'mkdir d1 d2; touch f1\nfor d in */; do echo "dir: ${d%/}"; done' },
      { title: 'The variable keeps its value', cmd: 'for i in 1 2 3; do :; done\necho "i=$i"' },
      { title: 'Do not parse ls', cmd: 'touch "a b" c\nfor f in $(ls); do echo "[$f]"; done\necho ---\nfor f in *; do echo "[$f]"; done', note: 'The first splits "a b" into two items.' },
    ],
    see: ['while', 'break', 'seq', 'glob', 'arrays', 'special-parameters'],
  },
  {
    name: 'while', kind: 'syntax', aliases: ['until', 'while read', 'while IFS= read -r', 'while IFS=: read', 'while read -r', 'while IFS= read -r line', 'while loop', 'until loop', 'while true', 'while [ ]', 'while ((  ))', 'infinite loop', 'while read -r -d', "while read -r -d ''", 'while IFS=: read -r', 'while read line', 'read loop', 'while getopts', 'while shift', 'bucle while', 'while :', 'while IFS=, read'],
    summary: 'Loop while a command succeeds (`while`) or until it succeeds (`until`); the standard way to read a file line by line.',
    synopsis: ['while COMMAND; do COMMANDS; done', 'until COMMAND; do COMMANDS; done', 'while IFS= read -r line; do ...; done < FILE'],
    desc: ['`while` evaluates a command and repeats the body **as long as its exit status is 0**; `until` repeats **until it becomes 0** (while it fails). The condition can be any command: `[ ]`, `(( ))`, `read`, `grep -q`, `kill -0 PID`... `while true` / `while :` makes an infinite loop that you leave with `break` (or `exit`). **`break`** leaves the loop (`break N`: N levels), **`continue`** goes to the next iteration.', 'The most important idiom is **`while IFS= read -r line; do ...; done < file`**: `read` returns failure at the end of the file, which ends the loop; `IFS=` keeps leading/trailing blanks and `-r` keeps backslashes. `IFS=: read -r a b c` splits each line into fields. **The loop body runs in the current shell only when the input is redirected** (`done < file`, or `< <(cmd)`); with `cmd | while read ...` the loop is a **subshell** and variables set in it are lost. `read -d \'\'` with `find -print0` reads NUL-separated names safely.'],
    examples: [
      { title: 'A counter', cmd: 'i=1\nwhile [ $i -le 5 ]; do\n  echo -n "$i "\n  i=$((i + 1))\ndone; echo' },
      { title: 'until', cmd: 'n=3\nuntil [ $n -eq 0 ]; do echo "countdown $n"; n=$((n - 1)); done; echo "liftoff"' },
      { title: 'Read a file line by line', cmd: 'printf "first line\\n  indented line\\nlast\\n" > f\nwhile IFS= read -r line; do\n  echo "[$line]"\ndone < f' },
      { title: 'Split each line into fields', cmd: 'printf "ana:30:madrid\\nluis:25:lima\\n" > people\nwhile IFS=: read -r name age city; do\n  echo "$name is $age and lives in $city"\ndone < people' },
      { title: 'Number the lines / count them', cmd: 'printf "a\\nb\\nc\\n" > f\nn=0\nwhile IFS= read -r line; do n=$((n+1)); echo "$n: $line"; done < f\necho "total: $n"' },
      { title: 'The pipe trap: variables are lost', cmd: 'printf "a\\nb\\nc\\n" > f\nn=0\ncat f | while read -r l; do n=$((n+1)); done\necho "pipe: n=$n"\nn=0\nwhile read -r l; do n=$((n+1)); done < f\necho "redirect: n=$n"', note: 'A pipe makes the loop a subshell.' },
      { title: 'Loop over a command output', cmd: 'while IFS= read -r line; do echo "got: $line"; done < <(printf "x\\ny\\n")' },
      { title: 'A last line without a newline', cmd: 'printf "one\\ntwo" > f\nwhile IFS= read -r line; do echo "[$line]"; done < f\necho "---"\nwhile IFS= read -r line || [ -n "$line" ]; do echo "[$line]"; done < f', note: '`read` fails on the last unterminated line even though it read it: `|| [ -n "$line" ]` handles it.' },
      { title: 'Infinite loop with break', cmd: 'i=0\nwhile true; do\n  i=$((i+1))\n  [ $i -ge 4 ] && break\n  echo "iteration $i"\ndone' },
      { title: 'continue', cmd: 'i=0\nwhile [ $i -lt 6 ]; do\n  i=$((i+1))\n  (( i % 2 == 0 )) && continue\n  echo "odd: $i"\ndone' },
      { title: 'Wait for something', cmd: '(sleep 0.3; touch ready) &\nuntil [ -e ready ]; do sleep 0.1; done\necho "ready appeared"\nwait' },
      { title: 'Retry a command', cmd: 'tries=0\nuntil [ $tries -ge 3 ] || false; do tries=$((tries+1)); echo "attempt $tries"; done' },
      { title: 'Process the arguments with shift', cmd: 'set -- a b c\nwhile [ $# -gt 0 ]; do echo "arg: $1"; shift; done' },
      { title: 'File names with strange characters (find -print0)', cmd: 'touch "a b" $\'c\\nd\'\nfind . -type f -print0 | while IFS= read -r -d \'\' f; do echo "found [${f#./}]" | tr "\\n" "~"; echo; done | sort', note: 'NUL separates the names so even newlines in names are safe.' },
      { title: 'Arithmetic condition', cmd: 'n=10; steps=0\nwhile (( n > 1 )); do (( n % 2 == 0 )) && n=$((n/2)) || n=$((3*n+1)); steps=$((steps+1)); done\necho "steps: $steps"' },
      { title: 'Read from the keyboard until a word', cmd: 'printf "ana\\nluis\\nquit\\nbea\\n" | while read -r w; do [ "$w" = quit ] && break; echo "name: $w"; done' },
    ],
    see: ['read', 'for', 'test', 'break', 'process-substitution', 'find'],
  },
  {
    name: 'break', kind: 'builtin', aliases: ['continue', 'break N', 'continue N', 'break 2', 'break and continue'],
    summary: 'Leave a loop (`break`) or skip to its next iteration (`continue`).',
    synopsis: ['break [N]', 'continue [N]'],
    desc: ['`break` immediately ends the innermost `for`, `while`, `until` or `select` loop and execution goes on after `done`. **`continue`** skips the rest of the body and starts the next iteration (re-testing the condition). Both accept a number N to act on the N-th enclosing loop: `break 2` leaves two nested loops. Outside a loop they do nothing useful (a message in bash). In a `case` inside a loop they refer to the loop.'],
    examples: [
      { title: 'break: stop at the first match', cmd: 'for f in a.txt b.log c.txt; do\n  case $f in *.log) echo "found $f"; break ;; esac\ndone' },
      { title: 'continue: skip some items', cmd: 'for n in 1 2 3 4 5 6; do\n  (( n % 3 == 0 )) && continue\n  echo -n "$n "\ndone; echo' },
      { title: 'break 2 leaves both loops', cmd: 'for i in 1 2 3; do\n  for j in 1 2 3; do\n    (( i * j == 4 )) && { echo "found $i x $j"; break 2; }\n  done\ndone' },
      { title: 'In a while true loop', cmd: 'n=0\nwhile true; do\n  n=$((n+1))\n  [ $n -eq 3 ] && break\ndone\necho "stopped at $n"' },
      { title: 'continue in a while loop must update the counter first', cmd: 'i=0\nwhile [ $i -lt 5 ]; do\n  i=$((i+1))\n  [ $i -eq 3 ] && continue\n  echo -n "$i "\ndone; echo' },
    ],
    see: ['for', 'while', 'case'],
  },
  {
    name: 'select', kind: 'syntax', aliases: ['select loop', 'PS3', 'select menu', 'menu'],
    summary: 'Build a numbered menu: `select NAME in LIST; do ...; done`.',
    synopsis: ['select NAME in WORD...; do COMMANDS; done'],
    desc: ['`select` prints the words of LIST as a **numbered menu** on stderr, shows the prompt `$PS3` (default `#?`), reads a line from stdin and sets NAME to the word whose number was typed (`$REPLY` holds the raw text). The body runs for each choice; **there is no automatic end**: leave with `break` (or end of input, `Ctrl-D`). An invalid number sets NAME to empty. It is a quick way to make interactive menus without parsing input.'],
    examples: [
      { title: 'A menu (the answers come from the keyboard)', cmd: 'PS3="Choose: "\nselect fruit in apple pear quit; do\n  case $fruit in\n    quit) break ;;\n    "")   echo "invalid choice: $REPLY" ;;\n    *)    echo "you chose $fruit" ;;\n  esac\ndone < <(printf "1\\n9\\n2\\n3\\n")', note: 'In a terminal you type the numbers; here they are supplied by `printf`. The menu goes to stderr.' },
      { title: 'Menu from files', cmd: 'touch a.txt b.txt\nselect f in *.txt; do echo "selected ${f:-nothing}"; break; done < <(echo 2) 2> /dev/null' },
    ],
    see: ['case', 'while', 'read'],
  },
  {
    name: 'functions', kind: 'syntax', aliases: ['function', 'function name', 'name() {', 'name() { }', 'local', 'return value', 'function arguments', 'funciones', 'funcs', 'define a function', 'local variables', 'FUNCNAME', 'function return'],
    summary: 'Define reusable blocks of commands: `name() { ...; }`, with arguments `$1..`, `local` variables and `return`.',
    synopsis: ['name() { COMMANDS; }', 'function name { COMMANDS; }', 'name ARG1 ARG2          # call it like a command', 'local VAR=value   return [N]'],
    desc: [
      'A **function** groups commands under a name; you call it like any command, and it receives its own **arguments** in `$1`, `$2`, `$#`, `"$@"` (the script\'s positional parameters are hidden during the call; `$0` is still the script). It runs in the **current shell**, so it can change global variables and directories. **`local var`** makes a variable visible only inside the function (and the functions it calls): use it for every temporary variable. Define a function **before** the line that calls it.',
      'A function does **not return values**: it returns an **exit status** with `return N` (0–255, default the status of its last command) and prints results on **stdout**, which the caller captures with `$(func args)`. Conventions: a function used as a test returns 0/1 (`is_dir() { [ -d "$1" ]; }`, `if is_dir x; then`); a function that computes something `echo`s it. `exit` inside a function ends the **whole script**. Functions can call themselves (**recursion**) and be exported (`export -f`). `unset -f name` removes one; `declare -f name` prints its definition.',
    ],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Define and call', cmd: 'greet() {\n  echo "Hello, $1!"\n}\ngreet Ana\ngreet "Mr Smith"' },
      { title: 'Arguments inside a function', cmd: 'show() { echo "got $# argument(s): $@; first: $1"; }\nshow a b c\nshow' },
      { title: 'local variables', cmd: 'x=global\nf() { local x=inside; echo "in f: $x"; }\nf\necho "after: $x"\ng() { x=changed; }\ng\necho "g changed it: $x"' },
      { title: 'Return a status and use it', cmd: 'is_number() { [[ $1 =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; }\nfor v in 42 4x; do\n  if is_number "$v"; then echo "$v: yes"; else echo "$v: no"; fi\ndone\nis_number 7 && echo "status was $?"' },
      { title: 'Return a value through stdout', cmd: 'add() { echo $(( $1 + $2 )); }\nresult=$(add 2 3)\necho "result: $result"\necho "double: $(( $(add 4 5) * 2 ))"' },
      { title: 'return N', cmd: 'check() { [ -e "$1" ] || return 2; return 0; }\ncheck /etc/passwd; echo "$?"\ncheck /nonexistent; echo "$?"' },
      { title: 'Recursion', cmd: 'fact() {\n  local n=$1\n  if (( n <= 1 )); then echo 1\n  else echo $(( n * $(fact $((n - 1))) ))\n  fi\n}\nfact 5\nfact 10' },
      { title: 'Validate arguments and complain', cmd: 'need_file() {\n  [ $# -eq 1 ] || { echo "usage: need_file FILE" >&2; return 2; }\n  [ -f "$1" ] || { echo "$1: not a file" >&2; return 1; }\n  echo "$1 ok"\n}\nneed_file; echo "status: $?"\nneed_file /nonexistent; echo "status: $?"\nneed_file /etc/passwd; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'A function using the script arguments', cmd: 'cat > s.sh << \'EOF\'\nusage() { echo "usage: $0 NAME" >&2; exit 1; }\nmain() { echo "Hello, $1 (script arg 1 is $1)"; }\n[ $# -eq 1 ] || usage\nmain "$1"\nEOF\nbash s.sh; echo "status: $?"\nbash s.sh Ana', fails: true, note: 'Inside `main`, `$1` is what was *passed to main*; pass `"$@"` or `"$1"` explicitly.' },
      { title: 'Functions can be piped and redirected', cmd: 'upper() { tr a-z A-Z; }\necho "shout" | upper\nlist() { printf "%s\\n" one two; }\nlist > f; cat f' },
      { title: 'Use a function in a loop', cmd: 'square() { echo $(( $1 * $1 )); }\nfor i in 1 2 3 4; do echo "$i^2 = $(square $i)"; done' },
      { title: 'Count things with a global result', cmd: 'count=0\ninc() { count=$((count + 1)); }\ninc; inc; inc\necho "count: $count"\necho "in a subshell: $(inc; echo $count)"\necho "count after: $count"', note: '`$(inc)` runs in a subshell: its change is lost.' },
      { title: 'See and remove functions', cmd: 'hello() { echo hi; }\ndeclare -F | grep -c " hello"\nunset -f hello\nhello 2>&1 | head -n 1' },
      { title: 'Call before the definition: error', cmd: 'later; echo "status: $?"\nlater() { echo defined; }\nlater', fails: true },
      { title: 'The status of a function is its last command', cmd: 'f() { true; false; }\ng() { false; true; }\nf; echo "f: $?"\ng; echo "g: $?"' },
      { title: 'Fibonacci with a loop (iterative)', cmd: 'fib() { local a=0 b=1 i; for (( i=0; i<$1; i++ )); do local t=$((a+b)); a=$b; b=$t; done; echo $a; }\nfib 10\nfib 20' },
    ],
    notes: ['A function name that is also a command hides the command: `ls() { ...; }` — use `command ls` to bypass it.', 'Without `local`, every variable is global: a classic source of bugs in loops with `i`.', '`return` outside a function (or sourced script) is an error.'],
    see: ['exit-status', 'special-parameters', 'declare', 'source', 'script'],
  },
  {
    name: 'shift', kind: 'builtin', aliases: ['shift N', 'shift 2'],
    summary: 'Discard the first positional parameters: `$2` becomes `$1`, and so on.',
    synopsis: ['shift [N]'],
    desc: ['`shift` removes `$1` (or the first N arguments) and renumbers the rest: the old `$2` is the new `$1`, and `$#` decreases. It is how a script **consumes** its arguments one at a time (`while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do ...; shift; done`), takes **options first and the rest as files** (`shift` after processing each option) or handles a first fixed argument and loops on the others. Shifting more than `$#` is an error (status 1).'],
    examples: [
      { title: 'One at a time', cmd: 'set -- a b c\necho "$1 ($# left)"\nshift\necho "$1 ($# left)"\nshift\necho "$1 ($# left)"' },
      { title: 'Shift by N', cmd: 'set -- a b c d e\nshift 2\necho "$@"' },
      { title: 'Loop until none are left', cmd: 'set -- x y z\nwhile [ $# -gt 0 ]; do echo "processing $1"; shift; done\necho "done, $# left"' },
      { title: 'A fixed first argument, the rest are files', cmd: 'cat > s.sh << \'EOF\'\ncmd=$1; shift\necho "command: $cmd; files: $*"\nfor f in "$@"; do echo " - $f"; done\nEOF\nbash s.sh list a.txt b.txt' },
      { title: 'Handle simple options by hand', cmd: 'cat > s.sh << \'EOF\'\nverbose=0\nwhile [ $# -gt 0 ]; do\n  case $1 in\n    -v) verbose=1 ;;\n    -o) out=$2; shift ;;\n    --) shift; break ;;\n    -*) echo "unknown option $1" >&2; exit 2 ;;\n    *)  break ;;\n  esac\n  shift\ndone\necho "verbose=$verbose out=${out:-none} files: $*"\nEOF\nbash s.sh -v -o res.txt a b\nbash s.sh a b', note: 'See also `getopts`.' },
      { title: 'Shifting too much', cmd: 'set -- a\nshift 3; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
    ],
    exit: ['0 on success, 1 if N is greater than `$#`.'],
    see: ['special-parameters', 'getopts', 'while', 'case'],
  },
  {
    name: 'getopts', kind: 'builtin', aliases: ['getopts optstring', 'OPTIND', 'OPTARG', 'getopt', 'options parsing', 'parse options', 'option parsing', 'getopts :ab:', 'getopts "ab:" opt'],
    summary: 'Parse the options of a script (`-v -o file`) in a standard way.',
    synopsis: ['while getopts OPTSTRING NAME [ARG...]; do case $NAME in ...; esac; done; shift $((OPTIND - 1))'],
    desc: ['`getopts` handles one option per call, setting NAME to the option letter, and returns failure when the options end. **OPTSTRING** lists the accepted letters; a letter followed by **`:`** takes an **argument**, which arrives in **`$OPTARG`**. If OPTSTRING starts with **`:`** getopts is *silent* and you handle errors yourself: an unknown option sets NAME to `?` (and `OPTARG` to the letter); a missing argument sets NAME to `:`. **`OPTIND`** is the index of the next argument to examine: after the loop `shift $((OPTIND - 1))` leaves only the non-option arguments in `$@`. Options can be grouped (`-vq`) and the argument can be attached (`-ofile`). It handles single-letter options only (use `getopt` for `--long`).'],
    examples: [
      { title: 'A script with options', cmd: 'cat > tool.sh << \'EOF\'\n#!/bin/bash\nverbose=0; out=""\nwhile getopts ":vo:h" opt; do\n  case $opt in\n    v) verbose=1 ;;\n    o) out=$OPTARG ;;\n    h) echo "usage: $0 [-v] [-o FILE] ARG..."; exit 0 ;;\n    \\?) echo "unknown option: -$OPTARG" >&2; exit 2 ;;\n    :) echo "option -$OPTARG needs an argument" >&2; exit 2 ;;\n  esac\ndone\nshift $((OPTIND - 1))\necho "verbose=$verbose out=${out:-none} remaining: $*"\nEOF\nbash tool.sh -v -o result.txt a b\nbash tool.sh file1 file2\nbash tool.sh -vo out.txt x' },
      { title: 'Errors in the options', cmd: 'cat > tool.sh << \'EOF\'\nwhile getopts ":vo:" opt; do\n  case $opt in\n    v) echo "verbose" ;;\n    o) echo "output: $OPTARG" ;;\n    \\?) echo "unknown option: -$OPTARG" >&2; exit 2 ;;\n    :) echo "option -$OPTARG needs an argument" >&2; exit 2 ;;\n  esac\ndone\nEOF\nbash tool.sh -x; echo "status: $?"\nbash tool.sh -o; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'OPTIND and the remaining arguments', cmd: 'set -- -a -b value file1 file2\nwhile getopts "ab:" o; do :; done\necho "OPTIND=$OPTIND"\nshift $((OPTIND - 1))\necho "files: $@"' },
      { title: 'Options stop at the first non-option', cmd: 'set -- -a file -b\nwhile getopts "ab" o; do echo "option $o"; done\nshift $((OPTIND - 1)); echo "rest: $*"', note: 'After `file` the `-b` is just an argument.' },
      { title: 'Grouped options', cmd: 'set -- -xvf archive.tar\nwhile getopts "xvf:" o; do echo "$o ${OPTARG:-}"; done' },
      { title: 'Without the leading colon getopts reports errors itself', cmd: 'set -- -z\nwhile getopts "ab" o; do case $o in \\?) echo "bad option (handled by us)";; esac; done 2>&1 | sed "s/line [0-9]*/line N/"' },
    ],
    exit: ['0 while an option was found, non-zero at the end of the options or on error.'],
    see: ['shift', 'case', 'while', 'special-parameters'],
  },
  {
    name: 'read', kind: 'builtin', aliases: ['read -r', 'read -p', 'read -s', 'read -a', 'read -ra', 'read -n', 'read -t', 'read -d', 'read -u', 'read -e', 'read -N', 'read line', 'IFS= read -r', 'read from keyboard', 'read from stdin', 'read REPLY', 'REPLY', 'read -r -d', 'leer', 'user input', 'prompt'],
    summary: 'Read a line of input and split it into variables (from the keyboard, a pipe or a file).',
    synopsis: ['read [-ers] [-a ARRAY] [-d DELIM] [-n N] [-N N] [-p PROMPT] [-t SECONDS] [-u FD] [NAME...]'],
    desc: ['`read` takes **one line** from standard input, splits it into **words** (at `$IFS`) and assigns them to the NAMEs: the first word to the first name, ..., **all the rest to the last name**. With a **single** name it receives the whole line (but leading and trailing blanks are trimmed unless `IFS=`). With no name the line goes to **`$REPLY`**. It returns **0** if it read a line, **non-zero at end of file** (or on timeout), which is what ends `while read` loops. Always use **`-r`** (raw: do not treat backslash as an escape) and, to keep spaces, `IFS= read -r line`.'],
    options: [
      { flag: '-r', desc: 'Raw: backslash is an ordinary character (always use it).' },
      { flag: '-p PROMPT', desc: 'Show PROMPT (on stderr) before reading, only if input is a terminal.' },
      { flag: '-s', desc: 'Silent: do not echo the typed characters (passwords).' },
      { flag: '-a ARRAY', desc: 'Assign the words to the elements of an array.' },
      { flag: '-n N', desc: 'Return after reading **N characters** (without waiting for Enter); `-N N` exactly N.' },
      { flag: '-t SECONDS', desc: 'Time out after SECONDS (status > 128).' },
      { flag: '-d DELIM', desc: 'End the "line" at DELIM instead of newline (`-d \'\'` = NUL).' },
      { flag: '-u FD', desc: 'Read from file descriptor FD instead of stdin.' },
      { flag: '-e', desc: 'Use readline editing in an interactive terminal.' },
    ],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Ask the user (input supplied here)', cmd: 'read -p "Your name: " name <<< "Ana"\necho "Hello, $name"', note: 'In a terminal the prompt is shown and you type the answer. Here a here-string provides the input.' },
      { title: 'Words go to the variables, the rest to the last', cmd: 'read first second rest <<< "one two three four five"\necho "[$first] [$second] [$rest]"' },
      { title: 'One variable takes the whole line (trimmed)', cmd: 'read line <<< "   spaced   out   "\necho "[$line]"\nIFS= read -r line <<< "   spaced   out   "\necho "[$line]"' },
      { title: 'Without a name: REPLY', cmd: 'read <<< "typed text"\necho "$REPLY"' },
      { title: 'Split on another separator', cmd: 'IFS=: read -r user pass uid gid rest <<< "ana:x:1000:1000:Ana:/home/ana:/bin/bash"\necho "$user $uid $gid"\nIFS=, read -r a b c <<< "1,2,3"; echo "$((a+b+c))"' },
      { title: 'Into an array', cmd: 'read -ra parts <<< "red green blue"\necho "${#parts[@]} words; the last is ${parts[-1]}"' },
      { title: 'Backslashes: always -r', cmd: 'read x <<< \'a\\tb\'; echo "$x"\nread -r x <<< \'a\\tb\'; echo "$x"' },
      { title: 'End of file: the status', cmd: 'read x < /dev/null; echo "status at EOF: $?"\nread x <<< "text"; echo "status with a line: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'Read a file line by line', cmd: 'printf "one\\ntwo\\n" > f\nwhile IFS= read -r l; do echo "[$l]"; done < f' },
      { title: 'A single key (-n)', cmd: 'read -n 1 key <<< "yes"\necho "first character: $key"' },
      { title: 'Timeout (-t)', cmd: 'sleep 5 | { read -t 0.3 x; echo "timed out: status $?"; }', note: 'A status greater than 128 means the timeout expired.' },
      { title: 'Read a password silently', cmd: 'read -s -p "Password: " pass; echo; echo "(${#pass} characters)"', norun: true, out: 'Password: \n(8 characters)', note: 'Nothing is shown while typing.' },
      { title: 'Confirm (y/n)', cmd: 'read -r -p "Continue? [y/N] " ans <<< "y"\ncase $ans in [yY]*) echo "continuing";; *) echo "cancelled";; esac' },
      { title: 'Several lines with read -u', cmd: 'printf "10\\n20\\n" > f\nexec 3< f\nread -u 3 a; read -u 3 b\nexec 3<&-\necho $((a + b))' },
    ],
    exit: ['0 a line was read; >0 at end of file; >128 on timeout.'],
    see: ['while', 'word-splitting', 'arrays', 'heredoc', 'redirection'],
  },
  {
    name: 'trap', kind: 'builtin', aliases: ['trap EXIT', 'trap ERR', 'trap INT', 'trap TERM', 'trap -l', 'trap -p', 'trap SIGINT', 'trap cleanup', 'trap RETURN', 'trap DEBUG', 'trap - SIGNAL', "trap ''", 'cleanup', 'signal handler', 'trap HUP', 'trap USR1', 'EXIT trap'],
    summary: 'Run a command when the shell receives a signal or exits: cleanup, handlers, ignoring Ctrl-C.',
    synopsis: ['trap \'COMMANDS\' SIGNAL...', 'trap - SIGNAL           # restore the default', "trap '' SIGNAL          # ignore the signal", 'trap -l | trap -p'],
    desc: ['`trap` registers COMMANDS to run when the shell gets one of the SIGNALs (names with or without `SIG`, or numbers) or one of the shell\'s own pseudo-signals: **`EXIT`** (when the shell ends, however: end of script, `exit`, error, Ctrl-C), **`ERR`** (a command failed), **`DEBUG`** (before each command), **`RETURN`** (a function or sourced script returns). The command string is in single quotes so it is evaluated **when the trap fires**, not when it is defined. `trap - SIGNAL` restores the default action and `trap \'\' SIGNAL` makes the shell **ignore** it. `SIGKILL` and `SIGSTOP` can never be trapped. The typical use: `trap \'rm -f "$tmp"\' EXIT` to clean temporary files, whatever happens. After a handler for a signal like INT or TERM finishes, the script **continues** unless the handler calls `exit`.'],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Clean up on exit', cmd: 'tmp=$(mktemp)\ntrap \'rm -f "$tmp"; echo "cleaned up"\' EXIT\necho "working with a temporary file"\n[ -e "$tmp" ] && echo "it exists now"' },
      { title: 'EXIT runs on errors too', cmd: 'bash -c \'trap "echo cleanup" EXIT; echo start; exit 3\'; echo "status: $?"\nbash -c \'trap "echo cleanup" EXIT; echo start; false; echo continues\'' },
      { title: 'Handle a signal', cmd: 'bash -c \'trap "echo got TERM; exit 0" TERM; sleep 60 & wait\' &\nsleep 0.5\nkill $!\nwait $!; echo "status: $?"' },
      { title: 'Ignore a signal', cmd: 'bash -c \'trap "" TERM; kill -TERM $$; echo "survived SIGTERM"\'', note: 'The empty handler makes the shell ignore it.' },
      { title: 'SIGKILL cannot be trapped', cmd: '{ bash -c \'trap "echo never" KILL 2>/dev/null; kill -KILL $$; echo "not reached"\'; } 2>/dev/null; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'Restore the default', cmd: '{ bash -c \'trap "echo trapped" USR1; trap - USR1; kill -USR1 $$; echo "not reached"\'; } 2>/dev/null; echo "status: $?"', fails: true, note: 'Without a handler USR1 kills the shell (138 = 128 + 10).' },
      { title: 'ERR: run something when a command fails', cmd: 'trap \'echo "line $LINENO failed with status $?"\' ERR\nfalse\necho "next"\nls /nonexistent 2>/dev/null', fails: true },
      { title: 'Show and list traps', cmd: 'trap \'echo bye\' EXIT\ntrap -p EXIT\ntrap -l | head -n 2' },
      { title: 'A handler that counts', cmd: 'cat > s.sh << \'EOF\'\nn=0\ntrap \'n=$((n+1)); echo "USR1 number $n"\' USR1\nkill -USR1 $$\nkill -USR1 $$\necho "done"\nEOF\nbash s.sh', note: 'Single quotes: the counter is evaluated each time the signal arrives.' },
      { title: 'Ctrl-C and kill in a script', cmd: 'cat > s.sh << \'EOF\'\ntrap \'echo "interrupted, cleaning up"; exit 130\' INT TERM\nfor i in 1 2 3; do echo "working $i"; sleep 1; done\nEOF\nbash s.sh & pid=$!\nsleep 1.5; kill -TERM $pid\nwait $pid; echo "status: $?"', fails: true, note: 'The handler runs when the current `sleep` ends. A real `Ctrl-C` sends SIGINT to the foreground script the same way (background jobs ignore SIGINT, which is why we send TERM here).' },
    ],
    exit: ['0 unless a SIGNAL is invalid.'],
    see: ['signals', 'kill', 'mktemp', 'exit', 'functions'],
  },
  {
    name: 'source', kind: 'builtin', aliases: ['source file', '. file', 'dot command', 'source script', 'source ~/.bashrc', '. ./env.sh', 'sourcing'],
    summary: 'Run a script in the current shell (so its variables, functions and `cd` stay).',
    synopsis: ['source FILE [ARGUMENTS]', '. FILE [ARGUMENTS]'],
    desc: ['`source FILE` (or the POSIX dot, `. FILE`) reads FILE and executes its commands **in the current shell**, not in a child process. Everything it defines — variables, functions, aliases, the current directory, `set` options — **remains after it finishes**. That is how `~/.bashrc` configures your session, how `export` files and libraries of functions are loaded, and how a project can change your `PATH`. The file does not need the execute permission. If FILE has no slash, `PATH` is searched (then the current directory). The status is that of the last command of FILE. In a sourced file, `return` stops it early.'],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Load variables and functions', cmd: 'cat > config.sh << \'EOF\'\nGREETING="hello"\nsay() { echo "$GREETING, $1"; }\nEOF\nsource config.sh\nsay Ana\necho "$GREETING"' },
      { title: 'Running it normally does not keep anything', cmd: 'printf \'VALUE=42\\ncd /tmp\\n\' > s.sh\nbash s.sh\necho "[$VALUE] $(basename "$PWD")"\nsource s.sh\necho "[$VALUE] $PWD"' },
      { title: 'The dot is the same', cmd: 'echo \'X=from_dot\' > f.sh\n. ./f.sh\necho "$X"' },
      { title: 'Arguments to a sourced file', cmd: 'echo \'echo "args: $# -> $1 $2"\' > f.sh\nsource f.sh one two' },
      { title: 'Exit status and return', cmd: 'printf \'echo "part 1"\\nreturn 3\\necho "never"\\n\' > f.sh\nsource f.sh; echo "status: $?"' },
      { title: 'Add to the PATH for this session', cmd: 'mkdir bin; printf \'#!/bin/sh\\necho my tool\\n\' > bin/tool; chmod +x bin/tool\necho \'export PATH="$PWD/bin:$PATH"\' > env.sh\nsource env.sh\ntool' },
      { title: 'Reload your configuration', cmd: 'source ~/.bashrc', norun: true, out: '', note: 'After editing `~/.bashrc`, `source` it to apply the changes without opening a new terminal.' },
      { title: 'A missing file', cmd: 'source nothing.sh; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
    ],
    see: ['script', 'export', 'functions', 'alias'],
  },
  {
    name: 'echo', kind: 'builtin', aliases: ['echo -n', 'echo -e', 'echo -E', 'echo "text"', 'echo $var', 'echo >&2', 'echo escapes', 'echo vs printf', 'echo *', 'echo ~'],
    summary: 'Print its arguments separated by spaces, followed by a newline.',
    synopsis: ['echo [-neE] [ARG]...'],
    desc: ['`echo` writes its arguments to standard output, **separated by single spaces** and followed by a **newline**. What it prints is what the **shell** passes: variables, globs and substitutions are expanded before `echo` runs, and runs of spaces collapse unless the argument is quoted (`echo a   b` → `a b`; `echo "a   b"` → `a   b`). `-n` omits the final newline; `-e` interprets escape sequences (`\\n`, `\\t`, `\\\\`, `\\033`...); `-E` (default) does not. Because `echo`\'s handling of options and escapes **differs between shells and versions**, use **`printf`** for anything where the exact output matters (and for text that may start with `-`). Writing an error: `echo "message" >&2`.'],
    options: [
      { flag: '-n', desc: 'Do not print the trailing newline.' },
      { flag: '-e', desc: 'Interpret backslash escapes: `\\n` newline, `\\t` tab, `\\\\` backslash, `\\a` bell, `\\0NNN` octal, `\\xHH` hex, `\\e` escape (colours), `\\c` stop output.' },
      { flag: '-E', desc: 'Do not interpret escapes (the default in bash).' },
    ],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Basics', cmd: 'echo hello world\necho "hello   world"\necho hello   world', note: 'Unquoted extra spaces disappear; quotes keep them.' },
      { title: 'No newline (-n)', cmd: 'echo -n "no newline"; echo " <- same line"\necho -n "a"; echo -n "b"; echo' },
      { title: 'Escapes need -e', cmd: 'echo "a\\tb\\nc"\necho -e "a\\tb\\nc"' },
      { title: 'Variables and substitutions', cmd: 'name=Ana\necho "Hello, $name. Today is $(date -d 2024-06-01 +%A)."\necho \'No $expansion here\'' },
      { title: 'It prints what the shell expanded', cmd: 'touch a.txt b.txt\necho *.txt\necho "*.txt"\necho ~ "~"' },
      { title: 'Write to a file / to stderr', cmd: 'echo "line 1" > f\necho "line 2" >> f\ncat f\necho "an error message" >&2 2>&1 | cat' },
      { title: 'Print a list, one per line', cmd: 'files=(a b c)\necho "${files[@]}"\nprintf "%s\\n" "${files[@]}"', note: '`printf "%s\\n"` is the right tool for one item per line.' },
      { title: 'A text starting with a dash', cmd: 'echo -n\necho "-n"\nprintf "%s\\n" "-n"', note: '`echo -n` is an option, not text; `printf` is safe.' },
      { title: 'Colours with escapes', cmd: 'echo -e "\\033[1;32mgreen text\\033[0m" | od -c | head -n 1' },
      { title: 'Empty line', cmd: 'echo "before"; echo; echo "after"' },
    ],
    notes: ['Behaviour of `echo -e` and backslashes differs in `sh`, `dash`, `zsh`: for portable scripts use `printf`.', 'It always exits with 0 (except write errors).'],
    see: ['printf', 'quoting', 'cat', 'redirection'],
  },
  {
    name: 'printf', kind: 'builtin', aliases: ['printf %s', 'printf %d', 'printf %f', 'printf %02d', 'printf %s\\n', 'printf %5s', 'printf %-10s', 'printf %x', 'printf %o', 'printf %c', 'printf %b', 'printf %q', 'printf -v', 'printf %%', 'printf format', 'printf %8s %d', 'printf %03o', "printf '%s\\n'", 'printf %s %d %02d %8s', 'printf >&2', 'printf %e', 'printf %5.2f', 'printf %*d', 'printf %(%F)T', 'printf %(', 'format string'],
    summary: 'Print formatted text, like C\'s printf: precise, portable and repeatable.',
    synopsis: ['printf FORMAT [ARGUMENT]...', 'printf -v VARIABLE FORMAT [ARGUMENT]...'],
    desc: [
      '`printf` prints the FORMAT string, replacing the **conversions** (`%s`, `%d`...) with the ARGUMENTS in order. Escape sequences in the format (`\\n`, `\\t`, `\\\\`) are always interpreted, and **no newline is added** automatically: write `\\n`. If there are more arguments than conversions the **format is reused** for the rest (`printf "%s\\n" a b c` prints three lines) — a useful trick; with no arguments the conversions print as empty/0. Because it behaves the same in every shell and has no options that get in the way of the text, it is the preferred tool over `echo` for scripts. `-v VAR` stores the result in a variable instead of printing it.',
      'A conversion is `%[FLAGS][WIDTH][.PRECISION]TYPE`. **Types**: `%s` string, `%d` / `%i` signed integer, `%u` unsigned, `%x` / `%X` hexadecimal, `%o` octal, `%f` decimal number, `%e` exponent, `%c` first character, `%b` string with backslash escapes interpreted, `%q` shell-quoted (safe to reuse as input), `%%` a percent sign, `%(FORMAT)T` date/time (bash). **Flags**: `-` left-align, `0` pad with zeros, `+` always show the sign, space, `#` alternate form (`0x`). **WIDTH** is the minimum field width; **PRECISION** after the dot: digits after the decimal point for `%f`, maximum characters for `%s`. `*` takes width from an argument.',
    ],
    sections: [{
      title: 'Common conversions', table: [
        ['Format', 'Argument', 'Output'],
        ['`%s`', '`hello`', '`hello`'],
        ['`%10s`', '`hello`', '`     hello` (right-aligned in 10)'],
        ['`%-10s|`', '`hello`', '`hello     |` (left-aligned)'],
        ['`%.3s`', '`hello`', '`hel` (at most 3 characters)'],
        ['`%d`', '`42`', '`42`'],
        ['`%05d`', '`42`', '`00042`'],
        ['`%+d`', '`42`', '`+42`'],
        ['`%x` / `%X` / `%o`', '`255`', '`ff` / `FF` / `377`'],
        ['`%#x`', '`255`', '`0xff`'],
        ['`%f`', '`3.14159`', '`3.141590`'],
        ['`%.2f`', '`3.14159`', '`3.14`'],
        ['`%8.2f`', '`3.14159`', '`    3.14`'],
        ['`%e`', '`12345.678`', '`1.234568e+04`'],
        ['`%c`', '`hello`', '`h`'],
        ['`%%`', '', '`%`'],
      ],
    }],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Strings and numbers', cmd: 'printf "%s is %d years old\\n" Ana 30\nprintf "%s\\n" "a line"' },
      { title: 'No automatic newline', cmd: 'printf "no newline"; echo\nprintf "one\\ntwo\\n"' },
      { title: 'The format is reused', cmd: 'printf "%s\\n" apple pear fig\nprintf "%s=%s\\n" a 1 b 2 c 3\nprintf "[%s] " 1 2 3; echo', note: 'One format, many arguments: it loops.' },
      { title: 'Padding with zeros', cmd: 'printf "%03d\\n" 7 42 100\nprintf "file_%02d.txt\\n" 1 2 10' },
      { title: 'Alignment: a table', cmd: 'printf "%-10s %5s %8s\\n" Name Age Salary\nprintf "%-10s %5d %8.2f\\n" Ana 30 1234.5 Luis 25 987.654', note: '`-10` left-aligns in 10 characters, `%5d` right-aligns, `%8.2f` two decimals.' },
      { title: 'Decimals', cmd: 'printf "%.2f\\n" 3.14159\nprintf "%.0f\\n" 2.5 3.5\nprintf "%5.1f%%\\n" 45.678', note: '`printf` rounds (to even, here); the `%%` prints a percent sign.' },
      { title: 'Bases', cmd: 'printf "%d %x %X %o %#x %#o\\n" 255 255 255 255 255 255\nprintf "%d\\n" 0x1F 010 2>&1', note: 'Arguments starting with `0x` / `0` are read as hex / octal.' },
      { title: 'Octal for permissions', cmd: 'touch f; chmod 640 f\nprintf "%03o\\n" 0640\nstat -c "%a" f' },
      { title: 'Characters and truncation', cmd: 'printf "%c\\n" hello\nprintf "%.3s|%5.2s|\\n" abcdef abcdef' },
      { title: 'Store in a variable (-v)', cmd: 'printf -v padded "%05d" 42\necho "$padded"\nprintf -v line "%s-%s" a b; echo "$line"' },
      { title: 'Safe quoting of arbitrary text (%q)', cmd: 'printf "%q\\n" "it\'s a file name.txt" \'$HOME\' "tab\\there"' },
      { title: 'Escapes in an argument (%b)', cmd: 'printf "%s\\n" "a\\tb"\nprintf "%b\\n" "a\\tb"' },
      { title: 'Text that starts with a dash', cmd: 'printf "%s\\n" "-n" "-e"\nprintf -- "-5\\n"' },
      { title: 'Date and time (bash)', cmd: 'printf "%(%Y-%m-%d)T\\n" 0\nprintf "%(%H:%M)T\\n" 3600', note: 'The argument is seconds since 1970 (`-1` = now, `-2` = shell start). Here UTC is used.' },
      { title: 'A computed width', cmd: 'printf "%*s|\\n" 8 abc\nprintf "%-*s|\\n" 8 abc' },
      { title: 'To stderr', cmd: 'printf "error: %s\\n" "bad input" >&2 2>&1' },
      { title: 'Missing arguments and bad numbers', cmd: 'printf "[%s] [%d]\\n"\nprintf "%d\\n" abc; echo "status: $?"', fails: true, note: 'Missing arguments print as empty/0; a non-number gives an error and 0.' },
      { title: 'A receipt', cmd: 'total=0\nwhile read -r item price; do\n  printf "%-12s %7.2f\\n" "$item" "$price"\n  total=$(echo "$total + $price" | bc)\ndone << EOF\nCoffee 2.5\nSandwich 5.75\nCake 3.2\nEOF\nprintf "%-12s %7.2f\\n" TOTAL "$total"' },
    ],
    exit: ['0 on success, non-zero on an invalid format or an invalid number argument.'],
    notes: ['Always give a format: `printf "$var"` misbehaves if `$var` contains `%` or backslashes; use `printf "%s" "$var"`.', 'Floating-point values depend on the locale for the decimal point (`LC_NUMERIC`).'],
    see: ['echo', 'bc', 'date', 'column', 'quoting'],
  },
  {
    name: 'set', kind: 'builtin', aliases: ['set -e', 'set -u', 'set -x', 'set -o', 'set +o', 'set --', 'set -f', 'set -n', 'set -v', 'errexit', 'nounset', 'xtrace', 'set -a', 'set -m', 'set +e', 'set +x', 'shopt', 'shopt -s', 'shopt -u', 'shopt -s nullglob', 'shopt -s globstar', 'shopt -s extglob', 'shopt -s dotglob', 'shopt -s nocaseglob', 'shopt -s nocasematch', 'shopt -s histappend', 'shopt -s expand_aliases', 'shopt -s failglob', 'shopt -p', 'shopt -q', 'debug', 'debugging'],
    summary: 'Change shell options (`set -e -u -x -o pipefail`, `shopt`) and set the positional parameters (`set -- a b c`).',
    synopsis: ['set [-abefhkmnptuvxBCHP] [-o OPTION] [--] [ARG...]', 'set +o OPTION      shopt [-su] OPTION', 'set -- ARG...     # replace $1 $2 ...'],
    desc: [
      '`set` has two jobs. **(1) Shell options**: a **dash** turns an option **on** (`set -e`), a **plus** turns it **off** (`set +e`; the notation is inverted!). **(2) Positional parameters**: `set -- a b c` makes them `$1=a $2=b $3=c` (and `set --` clears them). With no arguments it lists all variables and functions. `shopt` controls the other bash-specific options (`shopt -s` set, `-u` unset, `-q` query).',
    ],
    sections: [
      { title: 'The options that matter', table: [
        ['Option', 'Name', 'Effect'],
        ['`set -e`', '`errexit`', 'exit the script as soon as a command **fails** (not inside `if` conditions, `&&`/`||` lists, `!`)'],
        ['`set -u`', '`nounset`', 'using an **unset variable** is an error (exit)'],
        ['`set -o pipefail`', '`pipefail`', 'a pipeline fails if **any** command in it fails'],
        ['`set -x`', '`xtrace`', 'print every command (after expansion, prefixed with `+`) before running it: the debugging tool'],
        ['`set -v`', '`verbose`', 'print each line as it is read'],
        ['`set -n`', '`noexec`', 'read the commands but do not run them (syntax check)'],
        ['`set -f`', '`noglob`', 'disable pathname expansion (`*`, `?`)'],
        ['`set -C`', '`noclobber`', '`>` refuses to overwrite existing files'],
        ['`set -a`', '`allexport`', 'export every variable assigned from now on'],
        ['`set -m`', '`monitor`', 'job control (on in interactive shells)'],
        ['`set -euo pipefail`', '', 'the usual "strict mode" combination'],
      ] },
      { title: 'Useful shopt options', table: [
        ['Option', 'Effect'],
        ['`nullglob`', 'a glob with no match expands to **nothing** (not to itself)'],
        ['`failglob`', 'a glob with no match is an error'],
        ['`dotglob`', '`*` also matches hidden files'],
        ['`globstar`', '`**` matches any number of directories'],
        ['`extglob`', 'extended patterns (`!(*.o)`, `+(a|b)`)'],
        ['`nocaseglob` / `nocasematch`', 'case-insensitive globs / `case` and `[[ ]]` matching'],
        ['`histappend`', 'append to the history file on exit instead of overwriting'],
        ['`expand_aliases`', 'allow aliases in scripts'],
      ] },
    ],
    examples: [
      { title: 'set -x: see what runs', cmd: 'name=world\nset -x\necho "hello $name"\nls /tmp > /dev/null\nset +x\necho "tracing is off"', note: 'The lines starting with `+` are the trace (on stderr).' },
      { title: 'set -e: stop at the first failure', cmd: 'bash -c \'set -e; echo start; false; echo "never printed"\'; echo "status: $?"\nbash -c \'echo start; false; echo "printed anyway"\'', fails: true },
      { title: 'set -e does not apply in conditions', cmd: 'bash -c \'set -e; if false; then :; fi; false || echo "handled"; echo "still running"\'' },
      { title: 'set -u: catch typos', cmd: 'bash -c \'echo "[$undefined_var]"\'\nbash -c \'set -u; echo "[$undefined_var]"; echo "not reached"\' 2>&1 | sed "s/line [0-9]*/line N/"', note: 'Without `-u` the typo gives an empty string silently.' },
      { title: 'set -o pipefail', cmd: 'bash -c \'false | true; echo "without: $?"\'\nbash -c \'set -o pipefail; false | true; echo "with: $?"\'' },
      { title: 'The usual strict mode', cmd: 'cat > s.sh << \'EOF\'\n#!/bin/bash\nset -euo pipefail\necho "step 1"\nls /nonexistent 2>/dev/null | wc -l\necho "step 2 (not reached)"\nEOF\nbash s.sh; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'Set the positional parameters', cmd: 'set -- apple "red pear" fig\necho "$# arguments; second: $2"\nset --\necho "$# arguments now"' },
      { title: 'Split a string into arguments', cmd: 'set -- $(date -d 2024-06-01 "+%Y %m %d")\necho "year=$1 month=$2 day=$3"' },
      { title: 'noclobber', cmd: 'echo a > f\nset -C\necho b > f 2>/dev/null || echo "refused to overwrite"\necho b >| f; cat f\nset +C' },
      { title: 'noglob', cmd: 'touch a.txt b.txt\nset -f\necho *.txt\nset +f\necho *.txt' },
      { title: 'shopt: nullglob and dotglob', cmd: 'touch .hidden shown\nshopt -s dotglob\necho *\nshopt -u dotglob\necho *\nshopt -s nullglob\nfor f in *.nomatch; do echo "never"; done; echo "no match, no iteration"' },
      { title: 'Query and list options', cmd: 'shopt -q nullglob && echo "nullglob on" || echo "nullglob off"\nset -o | grep -E "^(errexit|nounset|pipefail)"' },
      { title: 'Check the syntax without running', cmd: 'printf \'echo hi\\nif true; then\\n\' > bad.sh\nbash -n bad.sh; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'Trace a whole script', cmd: 'printf \'x=5\\necho $((x * 2))\\n\' > s.sh\nbash -x s.sh', note: '`bash -x script` is the same as `set -x` at the top.' },
      { title: 'A better trace prompt', cmd: 'PS4=\'+ line ${LINENO}: \'\nset -x\necho one\necho two\nset +x' },
    ],
    notes: ['`set -e` has many exceptions and is not a replacement for checking errors in important places.', '`set -x` also prints secrets (passwords in arguments).', 'Settings made in a script are local to that script\'s shell.'],
    see: ['script', 'exit-status', 'glob', 'bash', 'trap'],
  },
  {
    name: 'bash', kind: 'command', aliases: ['bash -c', 'bash -n', 'bash -x', 'sh', 'sh -c', 'bash script', 'bash --version', 'bash -l', 'bash -i', 'bash -s', 'bash command', 'dash', 'zsh', 'login shell', 'interactive shell', 'non-interactive shell', 'bash -c "cmd"', 'login-shell', '.bashrc', '.bash_profile', '.profile', '~/.bashrc', 'bashrc', 'startup files'],
    summary: 'The shell itself: run a script (`bash script.sh`) or a command string (`bash -c "..."`); interactive vs login shells and startup files.',
    synopsis: ['bash [OPTION]... [SCRIPT [ARGUMENT]...]', 'bash -c \'COMMANDS\' [NAME [ARGUMENT]...]', 'bash -x|-n|-v SCRIPT'],
    desc: [
      '`bash` (the *Bourne-Again SHell*) reads commands from the terminal, from a script file or from a string, and runs them. **`bash script.sh args`** runs a script in a **new process** (no execute permission required, shebang ignored). **`bash -c \'cmds\' [name [args]]`** runs the string; the first extra word becomes `$0` and the rest `$1`, `$2`... **`bash -x`** traces, **`bash -n`** only checks the syntax, **`bash -v`** prints lines as read. **`sh`** is a POSIX shell (on Debian/Ubuntu it is `dash`, which lacks `[[ ]]`, arrays, `<( )`, `${var//}` ...): a script with `#!/bin/sh` must stay portable.',
      'A shell can be **interactive** (reads from a terminal and shows a prompt) or not (a script), and a **login** shell (first shell after logging in, `bash -l`) or not. **Startup files**: a **login** shell reads `/etc/profile` then the first of `~/.bash_profile`, `~/.bash_login`, `~/.profile`; a non-login **interactive** shell (a new terminal window, `bash`) reads `~/.bashrc`; a non-interactive shell reads none (unless `$BASH_ENV` is set). Put environment variables in `~/.profile` and aliases, prompt and functions in `~/.bashrc` (usually `.profile` sources `.bashrc`). `~/.bash_logout` runs when a login shell ends. A **subshell** is a child copy of the current shell, created by `( ... )`, `$( ... )`, pipelines and background jobs: changes made there do not come back.',
    ],
    options: [
      { flag: '-c STRING', desc: 'Execute STRING as commands. Extra arguments: `$0`, `$1`...' },
      { flag: '-x', desc: 'Trace: print each command with `+` before running it.' },
      { flag: '-n', desc: 'Read the script but do not execute it (syntax check).' },
      { flag: '-v', desc: 'Print each line of the script as it is read.' },
      { flag: '-e / -u', desc: 'The options `errexit` / `nounset` for the whole run.' },
      { flag: '-l || --login', desc: 'Act as a login shell.' },
      { flag: '-i', desc: 'Force an interactive shell.' },
      { flag: '-s', desc: 'Read commands from standard input (arguments become `$1`...).' },
      { flag: '--norc / --noprofile', desc: 'Do not read `~/.bashrc` / the profile files.' },
      { flag: '--version', desc: 'Show the version.' },
    ],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Run a script', cmd: 'printf \'echo "script: $0, args: $*"\\n\' > s.sh\nbash s.sh one two' },
      { title: 'Run a string (-c)', cmd: 'bash -c \'echo "$0 / $1 / $2"\' name first second', note: 'The first word after the string is `$0`.' },
      { title: 'A child shell cannot change the parent', cmd: 'x=1\nbash -c \'x=2; cd /tmp; echo "child: x=$x in $PWD"\'\necho "parent: x=$x"' },
      { title: 'Subshells with ( )', cmd: 'x=1\n( x=2; cd /tmp; echo "subshell: x=$x in $PWD" )\necho "parent: x=$x"\n{ x=3; echo "group: x=$x"; }\necho "after group: x=$x"', note: '`( )` is a subshell (separate); `{ }` a group in the current shell.' },
      { title: 'Syntax check and trace', cmd: 'printf \'a=2\\necho $((a + 3))\\n\' > s.sh\nbash -n s.sh && echo "syntax ok"\nbash -x s.sh' },
      { title: 'Which shell is /bin/sh?', cmd: 'ls -l /bin/sh | cut -d">" -f2\nbash --version | head -n 1 | cut -d"(" -f1' },
      { title: 'bash features missing in sh', cmd: 'sh -c \'[[ a == a ]] && echo "bash only"\' 2>&1 | head -n 1\nbash -c \'[[ a == a ]] && echo "works in bash"\'', note: 'On Debian/Ubuntu `sh` is `dash`.' },
      { title: 'Interactive or not', cmd: '[[ $- == *i* ]] && echo "interactive" || echo "not interactive (a script or bash -c)"\nbash -c \'[[ -t 0 ]] && echo "stdin is a terminal" || echo "stdin is not a terminal"\' < /dev/null', note: 'The `i` in `$-` marks an interactive shell.' },
      { title: 'Login and non-login', cmd: 'bash -c \'shopt -q login_shell && echo login || echo "not a login shell"\'\nbash -lc \'shopt -q login_shell && echo login || echo "not a login shell"\'' },
      { title: 'Read the script from stdin', cmd: 'echo \'echo "from stdin: $1"\' | bash -s argument' },
      { title: 'Where to put what', cmd: 'cat >> ~/.bashrc << \'EOF\'\nalias ll="ls -l"\nexport EDITOR=nano\nEOF', norun: true, out: '', note: 'Then `source ~/.bashrc` or open a new terminal.' },
    ],
    see: ['script', 'source', 'set', 'exec', 'export', 'alias'],
  },
  {
    name: ':', key: 'colon', kind: 'builtin', aliases: [':', 'true', 'false', 'no-op', 'null command', ': > file', ': ${VAR:=default}'],
    summary: '`:` (colon), `true` and `false`: commands that do nothing and succeed (`:`, `true`) or fail (`false`).',
    synopsis: [':  [ARGS]', 'true', 'false'],
    desc: ['`true` does nothing and returns **0**; `false` does nothing and returns **1**; `:` (the "null command") also does nothing and returns 0, but ignores its arguments after **expanding** them (so `: ${VAR:=default}` assigns a default without printing anything). Uses: an endless loop (`while true`, `while :`), an empty branch (`then :`), truncating a file (`: > file`), commented-out heredocs (`: << \'EOF\'`), and as a command that is always true/false in conditions (`cmd || true` so that `set -e` does not stop the script).'],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Their statuses', cmd: 'true; echo "true: $?"\nfalse; echo "false: $?"\n:; echo ":: $?"', note: 'Useful to understand `&&` and `||` (try `true && echo yes`, `false || echo no`).' },
      { title: 'An empty branch', cmd: 'if true; then\n  :   # nothing to do here yet\nelse\n  echo "never"\nfi' },
      { title: 'Endless loop', cmd: 'n=0\nwhile :; do n=$((n+1)); [ $n -ge 3 ] && break; done; echo "$n"' },
      { title: 'Assign a default silently', cmd: 'unset DIR\n: "${DIR:=/tmp/work}"\necho "$DIR"' },
      { title: 'Truncate a file', cmd: 'echo data > f\n: > f\nwc -c < f' },
      { title: 'Keep set -e from stopping', cmd: 'bash -c \'set -e; grep -q nothing /etc/passwd || true; echo "script continues"\'' },
      { title: 'A multi-line comment', cmd: ': << \'COMMENT\'\nthis text is ignored\nCOMMENT\necho "after the comment"' },
    ],
    see: ['if', 'while', 'exit-status', 'set'],
  },
  {
    name: 'subshell-and-group', kind: 'syntax', aliases: ['( )', '{ }', '( ... )', '{ ...; }', 'group command', 'subshell', 'subshells', '(cmd)', '{ cmd; }', '(cd dir && cmd)', 'command grouping', 'grouping commands', '; (semicolon)', ';', 'sequential', 'list of commands', 'agrupar comandos'],
    summary: 'Group commands: `( cmd; cmd )` runs them in a subshell, `{ cmd; cmd; }` in the current shell; `;` and newline separate, `&` backgrounds.',
    synopsis: ['( COMMANDS )        # subshell', '{ COMMANDS; }       # group, current shell (note the space and the final ;)', 'CMD1 ; CMD2   CMD1 & CMD2   CMD1 && CMD2   CMD1 || CMD2'],
    desc: [
      '**Separators**: a newline or `;` runs the next command when the previous ends; `&` starts it in the **background** and goes on; `&&` / `||` run the next one depending on the **status**; `|` connects them. A group makes several commands behave as **one** for redirection, pipes or conditions.',
      '**`( list )`** runs in a **subshell**, a child copy of the shell: variables set and `cd` done inside **vanish** when it ends. Great for temporary changes: `(cd /dir && make)`; `(umask 077; touch secret)`. **`{ list; }`** runs in the **current shell** (no copy): changes stay. Syntax traps: `{` and `}` are *words*, so they need **spaces** around them and the last command needs a **`;`** (or newline) before `}`. Both forms can be redirected as a whole: `{ echo a; echo b; } > file`, `( ... ) | sort`.',
    ],
    examples: [
      { title: 'A subshell does not change the parent', cmd: 'x=1\n( x=2; cd /tmp; echo "inside: x=$x $PWD" )\necho "outside: x=$x $(basename "$PWD")"' },
      { title: 'A group does', cmd: 'x=1\n{ x=2; cd /tmp; echo "inside: x=$x"; }\necho "outside: x=$x $PWD"' },
      { title: 'Run a command elsewhere without moving', cmd: 'mkdir work; echo data > work/f\n(cd work && ls)\npwd | xargs basename' },
      { title: 'Redirect several commands at once', cmd: '{ echo "header"; date -d 2024-06-01 +%F; echo "footer"; } > report.txt\ncat report.txt' },
      { title: 'Pipe the output of a group', cmd: '{ echo b; echo a; echo c; } | sort' },
      { title: 'The spaces and the semicolon in { }', cmd: '{ echo one; echo two; }\n{ echo no-semicolon\n}', note: '`{` and `}` are words: they need spaces around them, and the last command needs a `;` (or a newline) before `}`. `{echo hi;}` and `{ echo hi }` are errors.' },
      { title: 'Conditions with groups', cmd: 'false || { echo "failed"; echo "reporting"; }\ntrue && ( echo "in a subshell"; exit 7 ); echo "status: $?"' },
      { title: 'Sequence and background', cmd: 'echo one; echo two\nsleep 0.2 & echo "does not wait"; wait' },
      { title: 'Temporary umask / environment', cmd: '(umask 077; touch private)\ntouch normal\nstat -c "%a %n" private normal' },
      { title: 'Exit in a subshell', cmd: '( exit 3 ); echo "status: $?"\necho "script still running"' },
    ],
    see: ['if', 'bash', 'redirection', 'pipes', 'jobs'],
  },
]);
